# PARSE: matching and pulling input apart.
# Origin: REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3, section 15, corrected against R3.
#
# This is the feature REBOL is known for, and it is a sub-language of its
# own: a rule block is not evaluated, it is matched.
#
# Nine of these entries were wrong when they were written from the guide,
# because the guide documents REBOL 2 and two things changed underneath
# it. An integer in a rule is a repeat count and never a value to match,
# so [any 1] is "ANY, once" with nothing left to repeat rather than "any
# number of ones". And parsing a string no longer skips whitespace, so a
# rule has to account for the spaces itself. Every entry below was run
# against a real R3 (3.22.1).

# --- Whether the input matched ------------------------------

--- id parse/matching-literals-in-order
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15, corrected against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse "abc" ["a" "b" "c"]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/matching-a-string-does-not-skip-whitespace
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note REBOL 2 skipped whitespace unless you passed /all. R3 reversed the
--- note default and dropped the refinement, so this is now false where the
--- note guide says true. It is the second half of why the parse corpus was
--- note wrong.
--- code
parse "a b c" ["a" "b" "c"]
--- result
#(false)

--- id parse/whitespace-has-to-be-matched-explicitly
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse "a b c" ["a" " " "b" " " "c"]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/a-literal-that-does-not-match
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15, corrected against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse "ab" ["a" "c"]
--- result
#(false)

--- id parse/input-left-over-does-not-match
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15, corrected against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note A rule that runs out before the input does has not matched it.
--- note Matching means matching all of it.
--- code
parse "abcd" ["a" "b" "c"]
--- result
#(false)

--- id parse/an-empty-rule-against-empty-input
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse "" []
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/an-empty-rule-against-input
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- note The pair above and below are the boundary: an empty rule matches
--- note nothing, and matching nothing is only enough when there was
--- note nothing there.
--- code
parse "a" []
--- result
#(false)

# --- An integer is a count, not a value ---------------------

--- id parse/an-integer-in-a-rule-is-a-repeat-count
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note The difference that made nine entries here wrong. In the evaluator
--- note an integer is a value; in a rule block it is the number of times to
--- note apply whatever comes next. This is also the ON point for the count,
--- note with one short and one over below it.
--- code
parse [1 1 1] [3 integer!]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/a-count-with-no-rule-after-it-raises
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note This is what [any 1] actually means: ANY, once, with nothing left
--- note to repeat. The guide's [any 1] examples all raise.
--- code
parse [1 1 1] [any 1]
--- error script parse-end

--- id parse/a-bare-count-at-the-end-of-a-rule-raises
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1] [1]
--- error script parse-end

--- id parse/a-count-one-short-does-not-match
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note The OFF point below the count. Two integers matched, one left over,
--- note and leftover input is a failure.
--- code
parse [1 1 1] [2 integer!]
--- result
#(false)

--- id parse/a-count-one-over-does-not-match
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note The OFF point above the count. There is no fourth integer.
--- code
parse [1 1 1] [4 integer!]
--- result
#(false)

--- id parse/two-integers-are-a-range
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note Inclusive at both ends.
--- code
parse [1 1 1] [2 3 integer!]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/a-range-reaching-its-upper-end
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1 1 1] [1 3 integer!]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/a-count-of-zero-against-empty-input
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note Zero matches nothing, and nothing is enough when the input was
--- note already empty.
--- code
parse [] [0 integer!]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/a-count-of-zero-leaves-the-input-unaccounted-for
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note The same rule against non-empty input fails, not because zero
--- note failed to match but because the three integers are still there.
--- code
parse [1 1 1] [0 integer!]
--- result
#(false)

# --- Repetition by name -------------------------------------

--- id parse/any-matches-none-or-more
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15, corrected against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1 1 1] [any integer!]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/any-matching-nothing-still-matches
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- note The boundary that separates ANY from SOME.
--- code
parse [] [any integer!]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/some-needs-at-least-one
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [] [some integer!]
--- result
#(false)

--- id parse/some-matches-several
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1 1] [some integer!]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/opt-matches-one-when-there-is-one
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1] [opt integer!]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/opt-matches-none-when-there-is-none
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [] [opt integer!]
--- result
#(true)

# --- Moving through the input -------------------------------

--- id parse/to-stops-before-what-it-finds
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1 2 3] [to 3 integer! end]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/thru-goes-past-what-it-finds
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- note TO leaves the position before the match and THRU leaves it after,
--- note which is the whole difference between them.
--- code
parse [1 2 3] [thru 3 end]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/to-end-matches-whatever-is-left
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1 2 3] [to end]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/skip-passes-one-thing
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1 2 3] [skip skip skip]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/skip-past-the-end-does-not-match
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1 2 3] [4 skip]
--- result
#(false)

--- id parse/a-count-in-front-of-skip
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1 2 3] [3 skip end]
--- result
#(true)

# --- Alternatives -------------------------------------------

--- id parse/the-first-alternative-that-matches-is-taken
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [x] [integer! | word!]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/an-earlier-alternative-matching-is-enough
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1] [integer! | word!]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/no-alternative-matching-fails
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [#"a"] [integer! | word!]
--- result
#(false)

# --- Matching by datatype -----------------------------------

--- id parse/a-datatype-matches-any-value-of-it
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [when 10:30] ['when time!]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/a-datatype-that-does-not-match
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [when "not a time"] ['when time!]
--- result
#(false)

--- id parse/a-lit-word-matches-that-word
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [x] ['x]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/a-lit-word-does-not-match-a-different-word
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [x] ['y]
--- result
#(false)

# --- Keeping what was matched -------------------------------

--- id parse/set-keeps-the-value-it-matched
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- note This is what makes PARSE a way of pulling input apart rather than
--- note only a way of asking whether it fits.
--- code
parse [when 10:30] ['when set found time!]
found
--- result
10:30

--- id parse/copy-keeps-everything-a-rule-matched
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1 2 3] [copy taken some integer!]
taken
--- result
[1 2 3]

# --- Doing something while matching -------------------------

--- id parse/a-paren-runs-while-matching
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15, corrected against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
count: 0
parse [1 2 3] [some [integer! (count: add count 1)]]
count
--- result
3

--- id parse/a-paren-the-walk-never-reaches-does-not-run
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
ran: false
parse [2] ['nope (ran: true)]
ran
--- result
#(false)

--- id parse/a-paren-that-ran-stays-run-when-its-rule-fails
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note The edge the guide never mentions. The first alternative matches
--- note the integer, runs the paren, then fails on word!. Backtracking
--- note takes the second alternative but does not undo the paren, so the
--- note count is 1 even though that branch was abandoned.
--- code
ran: 0
parse [1] [[integer! (ran: add ran 1) word!] | integer!]
ran
--- result
1

# --- Nesting ------------------------------------------------

--- id parse/into-matches-inside-a-block
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [["Ukiah" 10:30]] [into [string! time!]]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/into-that-does-not-match-inside
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [["Ukiah" "not a time"]] [into [string! time!]]
--- result
#(false)

--- id parse/a-rule-can-be-held-in-a-word
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15
--- requires parse
--- note A rule is an ordinary block, so it can be named and reused. That is
--- note what makes a grammar out of a rule.
--- code
digit: [integer!]
parse [1 2] [some digit]
--- result
#(true)

# --- PARSE no longer splits ---------------------------------
#
# REBOL 2 and R3-Alpha both let the rule be a string, a character or NONE,
# meaning "split on these delimiters" rather than "match this grammar".
# 3.22.1 removed it: one name was doing two unrelated jobs, and SPLIT now
# does the second one.

--- id parse/a-string-rule-no-longer-splits
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15, corrected against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse "707-467-8000" "-"
--- error script expect-arg

--- id parse/a-none-rule-no-longer-splits-on-whitespace
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 15, corrected against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse "The trip will take 21 days" none
--- error script expect-arg

--- id parse/all-is-a-refinement-parse-no-longer-has
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note /all held whitespace out of the delimiter set, and went when the
--- note splitting form went. Asking for it is an error rather than a
--- note no-op, which is the behaviour worth pinning: a silently ignored
--- note refinement would let old code look like it still worked.
--- code
parse/all "a b c" ["a" "b" "c"]
--- error script no-refine

--- id parse/split-is-what-splits-on-a-delimiter
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
split "707-467-8000" "-"
--- result
["707" "467" "8000"]

--- id parse/splitting-something-with-no-delimiter-in-it
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note One piece, not none and not an error.
--- code
split "nodelimiter" "-"
--- result
["nodelimiter"]

--- id parse/splitting-empty-input-gives-no-pieces
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note No pieces rather than one empty piece, which is the opposite of
--- note what the entry below might lead you to expect.
--- code
split "" "-"
--- result
[]

--- id parse/two-delimiters-together-leave-an-empty-piece
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note Nothing between the two hyphens is still something: a piece, and
--- note an empty one. Together with the entry above this pins both ends of
--- note how emptiness is counted.
--- code
split "a--b" "-"
--- result
["a" "" "b"]

# --- Collecting while matching ------------------------------
#
# COLLECT changes what PARSE answers. Ordinarily a parse is a question
# with a logic for an answer; with COLLECT it is an extraction, and the
# answer is the block of everything KEEP matched.

--- id parse/collect-answers-what-keep-matched
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1] [collect [keep integer!]]
--- result
[1]

--- id parse/collect-with-nothing-kept-is-an-empty-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note An empty block rather than none, so a caller can append to what
--- note came back without testing it first.
--- code
parse [] [collect []]
--- result
[]

--- id parse/collect-does-not-mind-input-left-over
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note The consequence easiest to miss: the question is no longer "did
--- note this account for all of it", so leftover input is not a failure.
--- code
parse [1 2] [collect [keep integer!]]
--- result
[1]

--- id parse/keep-gathers-every-time-it-matches
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1 2] [collect [some [keep integer!]]]
--- result
[1 2]

--- id parse/keep-only-gathers-what-it-wraps
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note The SKIP consumes the word and keeps nothing, so only the integer
--- note comes back.
--- code
parse [a 1] [collect [skip keep integer!]]
--- result
[1]

--- id parse/keep-can-wrap-skip
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1] [collect [keep skip]]
--- result
[1]

--- id parse/keep-of-one-item-keeps-the-value
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note The count decides the shape, not the kind of rule: ANY that
--- note matched once keeps the value.
--- code
parse [1] [collect [keep any integer!]]
--- result
[1]

--- id parse/keep-of-several-items-keeps-a-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note And the same rule matching twice keeps them together, so a caller
--- note can tell one run from two.
--- code
parse [1 1] [collect [keep any integer!]]
--- result
[[1 1]]

--- id parse/keep-of-a-counted-run-keeps-a-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1 2] [collect [keep 2 integer!]]
--- result
[[1 2]]

# --- Looking without consuming ------------------------------

--- id parse/ahead-matches-without-consuming
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note The "a" is matched twice over: once to look and once to consume.
--- note Without the lookahead there would be nothing left for SKIP.
--- code
parse "a" [ahead #"a" skip]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/and-is-the-older-spelling-of-ahead
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse "a" [and #"a" skip]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/a-lookahead-that-does-not-match-fails-the-rule
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse "ab" [and #"b" skip]
--- result
#(false)

--- id parse/remove-takes-what-it-matched-out-of-the-input
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note The series is shorter afterwards, so this is a rule that changes
--- note what it is matching against rather than only reading it.
--- code
b: [1 x 2]
parse b [some [remove integer! | skip]]
b
--- result
[x]

--- id parse/removing-still-answers-whether-it-matched
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1 x] [some [remove integer! | skip]]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/remove-while-parsing-a-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note The same rule over a string. The string parser walked a copy of the
--- note text for a long time, so it had nothing to shorten and REMOVE
--- note worked on a block parse and not on this one.
--- code
v: "yx"
parse v [some [remove #"y" | #"x"]]
v
--- result
"x"

--- id parse/change-replaces-what-it-matched
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note REMOVE and an insertion in one step, so the input may be a
--- note different length afterwards.
--- code
s: "(1)"
parse s [change "(1)" "()"]
s
--- result
"()"

--- id parse/parsing-a-string-folds-case-by-default
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse "aAa" [no-case "AAA"]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/case-makes-the-rules-after-it-mind-case
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note A mode rather than a refinement, which is what lets one rule mind
--- note case in one place and not in another.
--- code
parse "aAa" [case "AAA"]
--- result
#(false)

--- id parse/case-still-matches-when-the-case-agrees
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse "aAa" [case "aAa"]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/if-fails-the-rule-when-its-paren-is-false
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note A guard: the rule matched, and IF decides whether that counts.
--- note Without it a rule cannot depend on what it has just captured.
--- code
parse [1 2 3] [collect [some [keep [set v integer! if (even? v)] | skip]]]
--- result
[2]

--- id parse/collect-nests
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note Each COLLECT produces a block, and an inner one's block is kept in
--- note the enclosing collection rather than merged into it. One flat list
--- note of gathered values cannot express this.
--- code
parse [] [collect [collect [collect []]]]
--- result
[[[]]]

--- id parse/an-inner-collect-keeps-its-block-in-the-outer-one
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1 2] [collect [collect [keep 2 skip]]]
--- result
[[[1 2]]]

--- id parse/two-keeps-in-one-collect-gather-together
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1 2] [collect [collect [keep integer! keep integer!]]]
--- result
[[1 2]]

--- id parse/keep-of-a-paren-keeps-its-value
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note And consumes nothing, so a collection can hold something that was
--- note computed rather than only what was matched.
--- code
parse [] [collect [keep (1)]]
--- result
[1]

--- id parse/keep-pick-gathers-items-one-by-one
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note Without PICK a run of two is kept as one block; with it the items
--- note go in separately.
--- code
parse [1 2] [collect [keep pick 2 integer!]]
--- result
[1 2]

--- id parse/keep-of-a-copy-keeps-the-copied-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note COPY captures a block however many items it took, so each keep
--- note here gathers a block of one rather than a bare integer.
--- code
parse [1 2 3] [collect some [keep copy w integer!]]
--- result
[[1] [2] [3]]

--- id parse/collect-set-binds-the-collection-to-a-word
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1] [collect set a [keep skip]]
a
--- result
[1]

--- id parse/collect-set-leaves-the-parse-answering-a-logic
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note The difference from a bare COLLECT, which answers the collection
--- note instead. With SET the collection has somewhere to go, so the
--- note answer goes back to being whether it matched.
--- code
parse [] [collect set a []]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/collect-set-still-minds-leftover-input
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note And because it answers a logic, the full-consumption rule that a
--- note bare COLLECT suspends applies again.
--- code
parse [1 2] [collect set a [keep skip]]
--- result
#(false)

--- id parse/a-second-top-level-collect-is-kept-in-the-first
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note The first COLLECT establishes what the parse answers, and any
--- note after it add their block to that rather than replacing it or
--- note starting a list of their own.
--- code
parse [1 2] [collect [keep integer!] collect [keep integer!]]
--- result
[1 [2]]

--- id parse/a-third-collect-goes-in-the-same-place
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
parse [1 2 3] [collect [keep integer!] collect [keep integer!] collect [keep integer!]]
--- result
[1 [2] [3]]

--- id parse/a-collect-that-is-not-first-in-the-rule-still-answers-alone
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note What comes before it does not make it a second collect: there is
--- note only one, so its block is the answer.
--- code
parse [1 2] [skip collect [keep integer!]]
--- result
[2]

--- id parse/a-bitset-matches-any-character-in-it
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse bitset
--- note How a rule says "any of these" without writing them out as
--- note alternatives, which for a set of any size is the only workable
--- note form.
--- code
b: make bitset! "ab"
parse "aab" [some b]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/a-bitset-does-not-match-a-character-outside-it
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse bitset
--- code
b: make bitset! "ab"
parse "aax" [some b]
--- result
#(false)

--- id parse/a-bitset-can-be-written-into-the-rule-as-a-construct
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse bitset
--- note A rule block is not evaluated, so `make bitset! "ab"` inside one
--- note is three values rather than a bitset. The construct is how it is
--- note written without a word to hold it.
--- code
parse "aab" [some #(bitset! #{00000000000000000000000060})]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/collect-into-inserts-at-the-position
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note Into an existing series rather than a fresh one, and at its
--- note position, so what was already there is pushed along rather than
--- note replaced.
--- code
a: [x]
parse [1] [collect into a [keep skip]]
a
--- result
[1 x]

--- id parse/collect-into-an-empty-series
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
a: []
parse [1] [collect into a [keep skip]]
a
--- result
[1]

--- id parse/collect-into-leaves-the-parse-answering-a-logic
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note As COLLECT SET does, and for the same reason: the collection has
--- note somewhere to go, so the answer goes back to whether it matched.
--- code
a: []
parse [1] [collect into a [keep skip]]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/collect-after-appends-past-what-is-there
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- note The counterpart to COLLECT INTO: INTO puts the collection at the
--- note series' position and pushes the rest along, AFTER puts it past
--- note what is already there.
--- code
a: [x]
parse [1 2] [collect after a some [keep skip]]
a
--- result
[x 1 2]

--- id parse/collect-after-an-empty-series
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires parse
--- code
a: []
parse [1] [collect after a [keep skip]]
a
--- result
[1]

--- id parse/removing-counts-as-getting-somewhere
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note SOME stops when a round gets nowhere. REMOVE leaves the position
--- note exactly where it was, so a walk that reads progress as "the
--- note position moved" decides it is looping and stops after one round.
--- note The input got shorter, which is the other way to advance.
--- code
v: "aa"
parse v [some [remove "a"]]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/what-removing-leaves-behind
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
v: "aa"
parse v [some [remove "a"]]
v
--- result
""

--- id parse/a-round-that-changes-nothing-still-ends-the-repeat
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Which is what keeps the rule above from running forever. SOME
--- note needs one round and no round succeeded.
--- code
parse "" [some [remove "a"]]
--- result
#(false)

--- id parse/removing-some-of-the-input-and-keeping-the-rest
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
v: "abc"
parse v [some [remove "b" | skip]]
v
--- result
"ac"

--- id parse/removing-from-a-block-parse-too
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
v: [1 2]
parse v [some [remove integer!]]
v
--- result
[]

--- id parse/removing-a-character-rule
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
v: "yx"
parse v [some [remove #"y" | #"x"]]
v
--- result
"x"

--- id parse/changing-a-match-to-nothing-is-a-removal
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
v: "yx"
parse v [some [change #"y" "" | #"x"]]
v
--- result
"x"

--- id parse/changing-to-something-longer
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
s: "abc"
parse s [some ["a" change "b" "XX" | skip]]
s
--- result
"aXXc"

--- id parse/what-change-copy-captured
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note COPY captures what the rule matched before CHANGE replaces it,
--- note so the word holds the old text and not the new.
--- code
s: "abc"
parse s [some ["a" change copy v "b" (uppercase v) | skip]]
s
--- result
"aBc"

--- id parse/a-set-word-marks-where-the-parse-has-reached
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note It consumes nothing and always matches. This is how a rule
--- note keeps hold of a position so a later rule can measure back to
--- note it, and Rebol's own SPLIT is built on the pair below.
--- code
parse "abc" [mk: skip]
mk
--- result
"abc"

--- id parse/the-mark-follows-the-position
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse "abc" [skip mk: skip]
mk
--- result
"bc"

--- id parse/a-get-word-seeks-back-to-a-mark
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The other half: the set-word records and the get-word returns
--- note to what it recorded.
--- code
parse "abc" [skip m: skip :m skip skip]
--- result
#(true)

--- id parse/break-stops-the-repeat-around-it
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note What matched before it stands; the repeat simply stops asking
--- note for more. Rebol's own SPLIT uses it to walk to a delimiter and
--- note stop there, and without it the walk runs to the end and the
--- note whole string comes back as one piece.
--- code
mold collect [parse "abc" [some [copy c skip (keep c) break]]]
--- result
{["a"]}

--- id parse/breaking-out-of-a-search-for-a-delimiter
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The shape SPLIT is built on: mark, walk to the delimiter,
--- note break, keep what lies between the two marks.
--- code
mold collect [parse "a-b" [any [m: some [n: "-" break | skip] (keep/only copy/part m n)]]]
--- result
{["a" "b"]}

--- id parse/a-rule-that-breaks-does-not-match-overall
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Because the input is not used up, which is the ordinary rule.
--- code
parse "abc" [some ["a" break | skip]]
--- result
#(false)
